\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Classification of AI-for-Life-Science scenarios represented by SciForge use cases. Scientific workflows are grouped into seven categories, each addressing a distinct research bottleneck; one representative task is selected per category to demonstrate how SciForge's modular substrate (Agent Runtime, Workflow Engine, Evidence DAG, Scientific Model Router) composes reusable services.}}{12}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:use-case-classification}{{2}{12}{Classification of AI-for-Life-Science scenarios represented by SciForge use cases. Scientific workflows are grouped into seven categories, each addressing a distinct research bottleneck; one representative task is selected per category to demonstrate how SciForge's modular substrate (Agent Runtime, Workflow Engine, Evidence DAG, Scientific Model Router) composes reusable services}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Automated research pipeline executed during the SciForge scenario-01 research sprint. The 132-stage, 197-commit agentic loop progresses from a biological research question through literature mining (DCT-M3), candidate gene curation, AlphaFold3 structural prediction, evidence triangulation, manuscript synthesis, and submission packaging. Quantitative metrics: 132 research stages, 197 Git commits, 6 parallel child-agent runs, 9 generated figures, $\sim $164 papers reviewed, and $\sim $85 evidence nodes produced. Selected artifacts and thread evidence are linked to versioned records, evidence summaries, and audit findings where captured.}}{13}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:research-pipeline}{{3}{13}{Automated research pipeline executed during the SciForge scenario-01 research sprint. The 132-stage, 197-commit agentic loop progresses from a biological research question through literature mining (DCT-M3), candidate gene curation, AlphaFold3 structural prediction, evidence triangulation, manuscript synthesis, and submission packaging. Quantitative metrics: 132 research stages, 197 Git commits, 6 parallel child-agent runs, 9 generated figures, $\sim $164 papers reviewed, and $\sim $85 evidence nodes produced. Selected artifacts and thread evidence are linked to versioned records, evidence summaries, and audit findings where captured}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.2}AI4AI: Automated Modeling for Scientific Discovery}{13}{subsection.4.2}\protected@file@percent }
\citation{vcbench2026}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Key biological conclusions from the meiosis initiation switch study produced by SciForge's agentic research sprint. The DCT-M3 causal-module triangulation framework decomposes the meiosis initiation switch into four regulatory layers: (1)\nobreakspace  {}permissive licensing---chromatin opening and epigenetic priming; (2)\nobreakspace  {}brake release---degradation of meiotic inhibitors via ubiquitin-proteasome pathways; (3)\nobreakspace  {}trigger execution---SPO11-mediated double-strand breaks and synaptonemal complex assembly; (4)\nobreakspace  {}chromatin/RNA competence---transcriptional activation, splicing regulation, and 3D genome reorganization. The study identified a candidate-gene atlas of 23 genes across 12\nobreakspace  {}molecular classes, supported by multi-omics evidence (transcriptomics, epigenomics, proteomics), AlphaFold3 structural validation, and systematic literature triangulation.}}{14}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:meiosis-conclusions}{{4}{14}{Key biological conclusions from the meiosis initiation switch study produced by SciForge's agentic research sprint. The DCT-M3 causal-module triangulation framework decomposes the meiosis initiation switch into four regulatory layers: (1)~permissive licensing---chromatin opening and epigenetic priming; (2)~brake release---degradation of meiotic inhibitors via ubiquitin-proteasome pathways; (3)~trigger execution---SPO11-mediated double-strand breaks and synaptonemal complex assembly; (4)~chromatin/RNA competence---transcriptional activation, splicing regulation, and 3D genome reorganization. The study identified a candidate-gene atlas of 23 genes across 12~molecular classes, supported by multi-omics evidence (transcriptomics, epigenomics, proteomics), AlphaFold3 structural validation, and systematic literature triangulation}{figure.4}{}}
